{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:3c5627d987b73aa7", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Bonds", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "When you buy a bond, you are lending to the issuer, which may be a government, municipality, or corporation.", "question": null, "answer": "A bond is a type of debt: when you buy one, you lend money to its issuer, which may be a government, municipality, or corporation. The issuer promises to pay interest at a specified rate during the bond’s life and to repay the principal when the bond matures, or comes due. Bonds also carry risks; for example, an issuer may fail to make interest or principal payments on time.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1932, "output_tokens": 215}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:3c5627d987b73aa7", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Bonds", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "When you buy a bond, you are lending to the issuer, which may be a government, municipality, or corporation. In return, the issuer promises to pay you a specified rate of interest during the life of the bond and to repay the principal, also known as face value or par value of the bond, when it \"matures,\" or comes due after a set period of time.", "question": "When you buy a bond, what are you lending, and what does the issuer promise in return?", "answer": "You are lending money to the bond issuer. In return, the issuer promises to pay interest at a specified rate during the bond’s life and repay the principal when the bond matures.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1932, "output_tokens": 233}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:3c5627d987b73aa7", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Bonds", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "If sold before maturity, the bond may be worth more or less than the face value.", "question": "Misconception: A bond always sells for its face value, even if you sell it before maturity.", "answer": "A bond’s sale price before maturity can be higher or lower than its face value. The source says that an investor receives the face value if the bond is held to maturity; that does not guarantee the same amount when selling early.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1932, "output_tokens": 195}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:c8bbe058816bfa4b", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Bond Interest, Principal, and Maturity", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "the issuer promises to pay you a specified rate of interest during the life of the bond and to repay the principal, also known as face value or par value of the bond, when it \"matures,\" or comes due after a set period of time.", "question": null, "answer": "A bond separates interest from principal. Interest is the payment the issuer promises during the bond’s life; principal is the amount, also called face value or par value, that the issuer promises to repay when the bond reaches its maturity date. Maturity is when the bond comes due after its set period of time.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1939, "output_tokens": 275}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:c8bbe058816bfa4b", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Bond Interest, Principal, and Maturity", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "the issuer promises to pay you a specified rate of interest during the life of the bond and to repay the principal, also known as face value or par value of the bond, when it \"matures,\" or comes due after a set period of time.", "question": "What does a bond issuer promise to pay during the bond’s life and at maturity?", "answer": "The issuer promises to pay interest during the bond’s life and repay the principal (also called face value or par value) when the bond matures.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1939, "output_tokens": 185}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:c8bbe058816bfa4b", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Bond Interest, Principal, and Maturity", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "Typically, bonds pay interest on a regular schedule, such as every six months.", "question": "Misconception: A bond’s interest and principal are both paid only when the bond matures.", "answer": "Interest is typically paid on a regular schedule during the bond’s life. The issuer promises to repay the principal when the bond matures.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1939, "output_tokens": 229}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:cf15fb1aaca96a93", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Corporate Bonds", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Corporate bonds are debt securities issued by private and public corporations.", "question": null, "answer": "A corporate bond is a debt security issued by a corporation to raise money. When you buy one, you lend money to the corporation. The issuer promises to pay interest during the bond’s life and repay its principal when the bond reaches maturity. Corporate bonds can have different credit ratings: investment-grade bonds have higher ratings and imply less credit risk than high-yield bonds, while high-yield bonds have lower ratings, imply higher credit risk, and offer higher interest rates in return for that increased risk.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1933, "output_tokens": 200}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:cf15fb1aaca96a93", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Corporate Bonds", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Investment-grade.  These bonds have a higher credit rating, implying less credit risk, than high-yield corporate bonds. High-yield.  These bonds have a lower credit rating, implying higher credit risk, than investment-grade bonds and, therefore, offer higher interest rates in return for the increased risk.", "question": "How do investment-grade and high-yield corporate bonds differ in credit risk and interest rates?", "answer": "Investment-grade corporate bonds have higher credit ratings and less credit risk than high-yield bonds. High-yield bonds have lower credit ratings and higher credit risk, and they offer higher interest rates in return for that increased risk.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1933, "output_tokens": 219}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:cf15fb1aaca96a93", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Corporate Bonds", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "Corporate bonds are debt securities issued by private and public corporations.", "question": "Misconception: Buying a corporate bond means you own part of the company.", "answer": "A corporate bond is a debt security, not a share of company ownership. When you buy one, you lend money to the corporation, which promises to pay interest and repay the principal when the bond matures.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1933, "output_tokens": 299}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:c8771f3fc563eb8c", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Municipal Bonds", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Municipal bonds, called “munis,” are debt securities issued by states, cities, counties and other government entities.", "question": null, "answer": "Municipal bonds, often called “munis,” are loans investors make to state, city, county, or other government issuers. The issuer promises to pay interest and repay the principal when the bond matures. Municipal bonds can be structured in different ways: general obligation bonds are payable from an issuer’s general fund or specified taxes, while revenue bonds are backed by income from a particular project or source. Interest from municipal bonds is generally exempt from federal income tax and may also be exempt from state and local taxes for residents of the issuing state.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1933, "output_tokens": 245}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:c8771f3fc563eb8c", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Municipal Bonds", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Municipal bonds, called “munis,” are debt securities issued by states, cities, counties and other government entities.", "question": "What are municipal bonds, and who issues them?", "answer": "Municipal bonds, often called “munis,” are debt securities issued by states, cities, counties, and other government entities.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1933, "output_tokens": 197}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:c8771f3fc563eb8c", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Municipal Bonds", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "Instead of taxes, these bonds are backed by revenues from a specific project or source, such as highway tolls or lease fees.", "question": "Misconception: All municipal bonds are repaid from taxes. Is that correct?", "answer": "No. Municipal bonds can be structured in different ways. General obligation bonds may be payable from an issuer’s general fund or specific taxes, while revenue bonds are backed by revenue from a particular project or source, such as highway tolls or lease fees.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1933, "output_tokens": 227}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:0f19249c2b98f941", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "U.S. Treasury Securities", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "U.S. Treasuries are issued by the U.S. Department of the Treasury on behalf of the U.S. government. They carry the full faith and credit of the U.S. government", "question": null, "answer": "U.S. Treasury securities are issued by the U.S. Department of the Treasury on behalf of the U.S. government. The source describes several types: Treasury bills are short-term securities that mature in a few days to 52 weeks; notes mature within ten years; and Treasury bonds typically mature in 30 years and pay interest every six months. TIPS are notes and bonds whose principal is adjusted based on changes in the Consumer Price Index, and they also pay interest every six months.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1936, "output_tokens": 334}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:0f19249c2b98f941", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "U.S. Treasury Securities", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Treasury Bills. Short-term securities maturing in a few days to 52 weeks\nNotes. Longer-term securities maturing within ten years\nBonds. Long-term securities that typically mature in 30 years and pay interest every six months", "question": "How do U.S. Treasury bills, notes, and bonds differ in their typical maturities?", "answer": "Treasury bills are short-term securities that mature in a few days to 52 weeks. Treasury notes mature within ten years, while Treasury bonds are long-term securities that typically mature in 30 years and pay interest every six months.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1936, "output_tokens": 229}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:0f19249c2b98f941", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "U.S. Treasury Securities", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "Treasury Bills. Short-term securities maturing in a few days to 52 weeks", "question": "Misconception: U.S. Treasury bills are long-term securities.", "answer": "Correction: Treasury bills are short-term securities, with maturities ranging from a few days to 52 weeks.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1936, "output_tokens": 439}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:3116d94378506e0f", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "TIPS. Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities are notes and bonds whose principal is adjusted based on changes in the Consumer Price Index.", "question": null, "answer": "Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities (TIPS) are U.S. Treasury notes and bonds whose principal is adjusted based on changes in the Consumer Price Index (CPI). They pay interest every six months and are issued with maturities of five, ten, and 30 years. In short, TIPS differ from bonds with an unadjusted principal because changes in the CPI affect their principal amount.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1936, "output_tokens": 241}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:3116d94378506e0f", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "TIPS. Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities are notes and bonds whose principal is adjusted based on changes in the Consumer Price Index.", "question": "What happens to the principal of Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities (TIPS) as the Consumer Price Index changes?", "answer": "The principal of TIPS is adjusted based on changes in the Consumer Price Index. TIPS also pay interest every six months.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1936, "output_tokens": 170}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:3116d94378506e0f", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "TIPS. Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities are notes and bonds whose principal is adjusted based on changes in the Consumer Price Index.", "question": "Misconception: Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities (TIPS) keep the same principal amount throughout their term, just like any bond. What is the correction?", "answer": "TIPS principal is adjusted based on changes in the Consumer Price Index, so it does not necessarily stay fixed throughout the term.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1936, "output_tokens": 213}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:70fba10b6c2e1865", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Investment-Grade vs. High-Yield Bonds", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Investment-grade.  These bonds have a higher credit rating, implying less credit risk, than high-yield corporate bonds.\nHigh-yield.  These bonds have a lower credit rating, implying higher credit risk, than investment-grade bonds and, therefore, offer higher interest rates in return for the increased risk.", "question": null, "answer": "Investment-grade and high-yield are two categories of corporate bonds. Investment-grade bonds have higher credit ratings and imply less credit risk than high-yield bonds. High-yield bonds have lower credit ratings and imply higher credit risk; they offer higher interest rates in return for that increased risk. The labels describe differences in credit rating and risk, not a guarantee that an issuer will or will not make its payments.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1940, "output_tokens": 408}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:70fba10b6c2e1865", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Investment-Grade vs. High-Yield Bonds", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Investment-grade.  These bonds have a higher credit rating, implying less credit risk, than high-yield corporate bonds.\nHigh-yield.  These bonds have a lower credit rating, implying higher credit risk, than investment-grade bonds and, therefore, offer higher interest rates in return for the increased risk.", "question": "How do investment-grade and high-yield corporate bonds differ in credit risk and interest rates?", "answer": "Investment-grade bonds have higher credit ratings and less credit risk than high-yield bonds. High-yield bonds have lower credit ratings and greater credit risk, and offer higher interest rates in return for that increased risk.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1940, "output_tokens": 220}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:70fba10b6c2e1865", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Investment-Grade vs. High-Yield Bonds", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "These bonds have a lower credit rating, implying higher credit risk, than investment-grade bonds and, therefore, offer higher interest rates in return for the increased risk.", "question": "Misconception: High-yield bonds have lower credit risk than investment-grade bonds.", "answer": "That’s reversed. High-yield bonds have lower credit ratings and higher credit risk than investment-grade bonds; they offer higher interest rates in return for that increased risk.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1940, "output_tokens": 217}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:df0c9bdf1fbdc90f", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "General Obligation vs. Revenue Bonds", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "General obligation bonds. Refers to bonds issued by a state or local government that are payable from either an issuer’s general fund or specific taxes (usually property tax). Revenue bonds. Instead of taxes, these bonds are backed by revenues from a specific project or source, such as highway tolls or lease fees.", "question": null, "answer": "General obligation bonds and revenue bonds differ mainly in what backs their payments. A general obligation bond is payable from a state or local government’s general fund or from specific taxes, often property taxes. A revenue bond is backed by income from a particular project or source, such as highway tolls or lease fees. Some revenue bonds are non-recourse, so bondholders may not have a claim on the underlying revenue source if that income dries up.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": false, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1938, "output_tokens": 418}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:df0c9bdf1fbdc90f", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "General Obligation vs. Revenue Bonds", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "General obligation bonds. Refers to bonds issued by a state or local government that are payable from either an issuer’s general fund or specific taxes (usually property tax). Revenue bonds. Instead of taxes, these bonds are backed by revenues from a specific project or source, such as highway tolls or lease fees.", "question": "What is the main difference between general obligation bonds and revenue bonds?", "answer": "General obligation bonds are payable from the government issuer’s general fund or specific taxes. Revenue bonds are backed by income from a particular project or source, such as highway tolls or lease fees.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": false, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1938, "output_tokens": 334}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:df0c9bdf1fbdc90f", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "General Obligation vs. Revenue Bonds", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "General obligation bonds. Refers to bonds issued by a state or local government that are payable from either an issuer’s general fund or specific taxes (usually property tax). Revenue bonds. Instead of taxes, these bonds are backed by revenues from a specific project or source, such as highway tolls or lease fees.", "question": "Misconception: General obligation bonds are always backed only by property taxes, while revenue bonds are backed by the issuer’s general taxes.", "answer": "Correction: General obligation bonds may be payable from the issuer’s general fund or from specific taxes, often property taxes; the precise payment source can vary. Revenue bonds are backed by revenue from a specific project or source, such as highway tolls or lease fees.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": false, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1938, "output_tokens": 354}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:8c6247750dd1f355", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Conduit Bonds", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "If the conduit borrower fails to make a payment, the issuer usually is not required to pay the bondholders.", "question": null, "answer": "A conduit bond is a municipal bond a government issues on behalf of a private entity, such as a nonprofit college or hospital. The private entity—the conduit borrower—typically agrees to repay the government issuer, which then pays interest and principal to bondholders. If the borrower misses a payment, the issuer usually is not required to make that payment to bondholders.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1934, "output_tokens": 205}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:8c6247750dd1f355", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Conduit Bonds", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "If the conduit borrower fails to make a payment, the issuer usually is not required to pay the bondholders.", "question": "In a conduit bond arrangement, what usually happens if the private entity borrowing through the government issuer fails to make a payment?", "answer": "The private entity (the conduit borrower) typically agrees to repay the government issuer, which pays the bondholders. If the conduit borrower fails to pay, the issuer usually is not required to make the payment to bondholders.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1934, "output_tokens": 180}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:8c6247750dd1f355", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Conduit Bonds", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "If the conduit borrower fails to make a payment, the issuer usually is not required to pay the bondholders.", "question": "Misconception: Because a government issues a conduit bond, it must repay bondholders if the private conduit borrower misses a payment. Is that correct?", "answer": "No. A government may issue a conduit bond on behalf of a private entity, such as a nonprofit college or hospital. The conduit borrower typically agrees to repay the issuer, but if that borrower fails to pay, the issuer usually is not required to pay the bondholders.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1934, "output_tokens": 236}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:7cc55783bc28ae80", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Credit and Default Risk", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The issuer may fail to timely make interest or principal payments and thus default on its bonds.", "question": null, "answer": "Credit risk is the chance that a bond issuer will not make interest or principal payments on time. If the issuer fails to make those payments, it defaults on the bond. The source also explains that high-yield corporate bonds have lower credit ratings and higher credit risk than investment-grade corporate bonds.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1935, "output_tokens": 221}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:7cc55783bc28ae80", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Credit and Default Risk", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The issuer may fail to timely make interest or principal payments and thus default on its bonds.", "question": "What does credit risk mean for a bondholder?", "answer": "Credit risk is the possibility that the bond issuer will not make interest or principal payments on time. If the issuer fails to make those payments, it defaults on the bond.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1935, "output_tokens": 156}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:7cc55783bc28ae80", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Credit and Default Risk", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "The issuer may fail to timely make interest or principal payments and thus default on its bonds.", "question": "Misconception: Because a bond issuer promises to pay interest and repay principal, bondholders are guaranteed to receive those payments.", "answer": "A promise to pay does not eliminate credit risk. The issuer may fail to make interest or principal payments on time and default on the bond.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1935, "output_tokens": 216}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d3d537bd170b52e6", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Interest Rate Risk", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Rising interest rates will make newly issued bonds more appealing to investors because the newer bonds will have a higher rate of interest than older ones.", "question": null, "answer": "Interest rate risk is the possibility that changing interest rates affect a bond’s value. When rates rise, newly issued bonds may offer higher interest than older bonds. As a result, an older bond with a lower rate may need to be sold at a discount before maturity. If held to maturity, the investor receives the bond’s face value plus interest.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1934, "output_tokens": 235}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d3d537bd170b52e6", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Interest Rate Risk", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Rising interest rates will make newly issued bonds more appealing to investors because the newer bonds will have a higher rate of interest than older ones.", "question": "If interest rates rise, why might an older bond with a lower interest rate have to sell at a discount?", "answer": "Newly issued bonds may offer higher interest rates, making them more appealing to investors than older bonds with lower rates. If you sell the older bond before it matures, you might have to sell it for less than its face value—a discount.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1934, "output_tokens": 245}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d3d537bd170b52e6", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Interest Rate Risk", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "Rising interest rates will make newly issued bonds more appealing to investors because the newer bonds will have a higher rate of interest than older ones.", "question": "Misconception: Because a bond pays a fixed interest rate, rising interest rates cannot affect the value of an existing bond.", "answer": "A bond’s fixed interest payments do not mean its value stays unchanged. When rates rise, newer bonds may offer higher interest, so an older bond with a lower rate may need to be sold at a discount before maturity. If held to maturity, the investor receives the face value plus interest.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1934, "output_tokens": 275}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:ac7b00c0bb85b50a", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Inflation Risk", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Inflation reduces purchasing power, which is a risk for investors receiving a fixed rate of interest.", "question": null, "answer": "Inflation risk is the chance that rising prices will reduce what a bond’s fixed interest payments can buy. A bond may keep paying the agreed interest, but that income may purchase fewer goods and services as prices rise. This is why a predictable payment amount does not guarantee the same purchasing power over time.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1933, "output_tokens": 259}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:ac7b00c0bb85b50a", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Inflation Risk", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Inflation reduces purchasing power, which is a risk for investors receiving a fixed rate of interest.", "question": "Why can inflation be a risk for investors receiving fixed-rate bond interest?", "answer": "Inflation raises prices and reduces purchasing power, so fixed interest payments may buy less over time.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1933, "output_tokens": 186}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:ac7b00c0bb85b50a", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Inflation Risk", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "Inflation reduces purchasing power, which is a risk for investors receiving a fixed rate of interest.", "question": "Misconception: If a bond pays a fixed rate of interest, inflation cannot reduce what those payments are worth.", "answer": "A fixed interest payment does not protect purchasing power from inflation. Inflation raises prices and can reduce the purchasing power of the interest an investor receives.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1933, "output_tokens": 190}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:c355086e2a61d601", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Liquidity Risk", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "This refers to the risk that investors won’t find a market for the bond, potentially preventing them from buying or selling when they want.", "question": null, "answer": "Liquidity risk is the chance that you may not find a market for a bond when you want to buy or sell it. As a result, you might be unable to complete the transaction at the time you choose. It is separate from the risk that the bond issuer might fail to make its promised payments.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1934, "output_tokens": 185}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:c355086e2a61d601", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Liquidity Risk", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "This refers to the risk that investors won’t find a market for the bond, potentially preventing them from buying or selling when they want.", "question": "What is liquidity risk for a bond?", "answer": "Liquidity risk is the possibility that you won’t find a market for a bond when you want to buy or sell it. This can prevent you from making the transaction when you want to.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1934, "output_tokens": 160}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:c355086e2a61d601", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Liquidity Risk", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "This refers to the risk that investors won’t find a market for the bond, potentially preventing them from buying or selling when they want.", "question": "Misconception: A bondholder can always sell a bond whenever they choose because there will always be a market for it.", "answer": "Correction: A bond may be difficult to buy or sell when you want. Liquidity risk is the possibility that investors will not find a market for the bond.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1934, "output_tokens": 174}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:a76797203fe9bb22", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Call Risk", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The possibility that a bond issuer retires a bond before its maturity date, something an issuer might do if interest rates decline, much like a homeowner might refinance a mortgage to benefit from lower interest rates.", "question": null, "answer": "Call risk is the possibility that a bond issuer will retire the bond before its scheduled maturity date. An issuer might do this when interest rates decline, similar to a homeowner refinancing a mortgage to benefit from lower rates. This means the bond may end earlier than its original term.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1933, "output_tokens": 234}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:a76797203fe9bb22", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Call Risk", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The possibility that a bond issuer retires a bond before its maturity date, something an issuer might do if interest rates decline", "question": "What is call risk in bonds?", "answer": "Call risk is the possibility that the issuer will retire a bond before its scheduled maturity date. An issuer might do this when interest rates decline.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1933, "output_tokens": 161}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:a76797203fe9bb22", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Call Risk", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "The possibility that a bond issuer retires a bond before its maturity date, something an issuer might do if interest rates decline", "question": "Misconception: Once I buy a bond, the issuer must keep it outstanding until its maturity date.", "answer": "An issuer may retire a bond before maturity, a possibility called call risk. The source notes that an issuer might do this if interest rates decline, so a bond may not remain outstanding for its full stated term.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1933, "output_tokens": 199}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:7b4b3c53cc4028f0", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Researching Bonds with EDGAR and EMMA", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The registration of these securities can be verified using the SEC’s EDGAR system. This information is available free of charge online at www.emma.msrb.org.", "question": null, "answer": "When researching bonds, use the relevant source for the bond type. For publicly offered corporate bonds, EDGAR can be used to check whether the securities are registered with the SEC. For most municipal securities issued after July 3, 1995, annual financial information, operating data, and certain event notices are available free through EMMA, the MSRB’s online system. If a municipal bond’s required information is not filed with the MSRB, treat that as a possible red flag—not, by itself, proof of fraud.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": false, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1940, "output_tokens": 495}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:7b4b3c53cc4028f0", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Researching Bonds with EDGAR and EMMA", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The registration of these securities can be verified using the SEC’s EDGAR system. Most municipal securities issued after July 3, 1995 are required to file annual financial information, operating data, and notices of certain events with the Municipal Securities Rulemaking Board (MSRB). This information is available free of charge online at www.emma.msrb.org.", "question": "Which resource can be used to check whether publicly offered corporate bonds are registered, and where can investors find disclosures for most municipal securities issued after July 3, 1995?", "answer": "Use the SEC’s EDGAR system to check the registration of publicly offered corporate bonds. For most municipal securities issued after July 3, 1995, annual financial information, operating data, and certain event notices are available free online through EMMA, the MSRB’s disclosure website.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": false, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1940, "output_tokens": 468}}
{"id": "6760ce50482a5675:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:7b4b3c53cc4028f0", "source_id": "6760ce50482a5675", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds", "topic": "Bonds", "subtopic": "Researching Bonds with EDGAR and EMMA", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "The registration of these securities can be verified using the SEC’s EDGAR system. Most municipal securities issued after July 3, 1995 are required to file annual financial information, operating data, and notices of certain events with the Municipal Securities Rulemaking Board (MSRB). This information is available free of charge online at www.emma.msrb.org.", "question": "Misconception: EDGAR and EMMA are interchangeable registries, so every bond should appear in both. What is the correction?", "answer": "EDGAR and EMMA serve different research purposes in the source: registration of publicly offered corporate bonds can be checked through SEC EDGAR, while most municipal securities issued after July 3, 1995 are required to file specified information with the MSRB, available through EMMA. The source does not say that every bond must appear on both systems.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": false, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1940, "output_tokens": 397}}
